<!--
    kuna: a callee's result, read untouched by the next call or by the
    RETURN, is that call's result although the callee's prototype is unknown,
    when one end of the hand-off says so.

    Stage / sub-stage: P4 return and parameter recovery, the ancestor walk
    behind the trials of the next call's inputs and the function's RETURN
    (Funcdata::ancestorOpUse, p4_calls/kuna_callresultuse.rs).  Option
    `callresultuse`, off|on|all, default off (kuna-callresultuse-armtail.xml);
    the script turns it on first.

    Source (clang -target aarch64-linux-gnu -O1 -fno-optimize-sibling-calls
    -ffreestanding; g and g3 are symbols without bytes, so their prototypes
    are unknown and they print as sub_2000 and sub_2100; g2 and b have bytes):

        long g(long);
        long g3(long);
        int *p;
        volatile long gv;
        long g2(long x) { return x + gv; }
        void b(void) { gv = 2; }
        long ret(long a) { return g(a); }
        long branch(long a) { long r = g(a); if (r) *p = 1; return r; }
        long chain(long a) { long r = g2(g(a)); *p = 1; return r; }
        long tail(long a) { return g2(g(a)); }
        void dead(long a) { g(a); *p = 1; }
        void fresh(long a) { g(a); g2(5); *p = 1; }
        void tovoid(long a) { g(a); b(); *p = 1; }
        void unknown(long a) { g3(g(a)); }

    The bug: x0 after `bl g` is an INDIRECT creation, the register g may
    return, and the ancestor walk refused such a value as a trial for the
    next call's input or the RETURN; and where no op names x0 (chain, tail:
    `bl g; bl g2`) the register was never heritaged, so the second call had
    no trial there at all.  branch printed `if (sub_2000()) ...` and void,
    chain and tail `sub_2000(); g2();`.

    `on`: the result handed to g2, whose body reads x0, is its argument
    (`g2(sub_2000())` in chain and tail).  b's body never reads x0 (tovoid),
    and in unknown neither g nor g3 says anything, so neither call gains
    one.  A result only handed back, or branched on and handed back (ret,
    branch, chain, tail, dead: x0 untouched at the ret) keeps the strict
    reading and stays void, since `void h(long a) { if (g(a)) *p = 1; }`
    compiles to branch's bytes too; fresh rewrites x0 before g2.  `all`: the
    untouched result at the ret is the return value (ret, branch, dead).
-->
